F = (__import__("pathlib").Path(__file__).resolve().parents[5].as_posix() + "/design/ecad/case/pcb/board-notes.md")
L = open(F, encoding="utf-8").read().split("\n")
i1 = [i for i, l in enumerate(L) if l.startswith("1. Build the case main board")]
assert len(i1) == 1
i1 = i1[0]
assert L[i1 + 1].startswith("2. Parts"), L[i1 + 1][:20]
assert "First power-up test" not in L[i1]
L[i1] = L[i1] + " (g) First power-up test of the case main board, before any wire is soldered: what is connected, the order, then one line per test pad and per rail, then what each hidden joint group shows and what the test does not prove (the indented lines below this step). The case has no firmware, so nothing here needs it. Keys: CS = design/ecad/case/sch-notes.md, CN = design/ecad/case/netlist.csv, BQ = design/datasheets/case_bq25895.pdf, ES = design/datasheets/case_esp32s3_mini1.pdf, SL = design/sim/circuit/pass-lines.md, SN = design/sim/circuit/sim-notes.md."
B = [
"   - (g1) Connected: a bench supply on the USB socket J1 only: its plus on the four VBUS pads A4, A9, B4, B9 and its minus on the GND pads A1, A12, B1, B12 (CS line 15, sheet 4; net VBUS_IN in CN), through a USB-C plug breakout. Voltage 5.0 V (the case's USB input is 5 V: `design/case/case-architecture.md` section 4 and SL row C2.3, \"5 V\"). Current limit 1.5 A, because the VBUS_IN tracks are sized for 1.5 A (board-notes.md, Round 1 part c, table, row VBUS_IN: \"1.5 A (input limit with a 1.5 A source\"). NOT connected: no cell on W5 and W6, no thermistor TH1, no pin board, no wire W1 to W4 or W7, no ring (the last four are not made yet at this step; board-notes steps 4 to 10). Meter: black probe on test pad TP16 (net GND, CN) for every reading; scope ground clip on TP16.",
"   - (g2) Order. First, unpowered, meter on resistance: between TP16 and each of VBUS_IN (pad 1 of C1), SYS (pad 1 of C4) and 3V3 (pad 1 of C9) there must be no steady low reading; a steady reading below about 50 ohm is a short (the short limit of step 9, lead), and then nothing is powered and the fault is found first. A reading that starts low and climbs is a capacitor charging (C1, C4, C9 are capacitors, CS section 2): wait for it to settle. The files give no expected resistance in ohm for these three rails: no expected value in the files (for the lead). Second, supply at 0 V, connected, then raise to 5.0 V and watch the supply's current display: the charger limits its own input to 500 mA by default (REG00 default 500 mA, as used in SL row C2.3 and BQ p.32), so a steady 0.5 A on the display means a short on the SYS side: switch off. The files give no expected idle current in mA: no expected value in the files (for the lead). Third, read the lines below, in the order written. Nothing is written to the charger: its registers stay at power-on values, and CE is held high by R21 so the charger does not charge (CS line 103, \"CE is high and the charger does not charge\"; this also means no cell is needed).",
"   - VBUS_IN, pad 1 of C1 (net VBUS_IN): the same as the supply's display. Accepted: above 3.6 V, the VBUS level at which the charger is awake with no battery (BQ p.7, V(VBUS_UVLOZ) \"VBUS for active I2C, no battery\", 3.6 V); the files give no drop figure for J1 and its tracks. A reading near 0 V: a J1 VBUS joint or its track is open.",
"   - SYS, pad 1 of C4 (net SYS), the charger's system output: 3.50 to 3.65 V at no load (BQ p.7, V(SYS(MIN)): \"3.50 3.65 V\", with VBAT below it, SYS_MIN 3.5 V). Instant-on with no battery is stated in BQ p.1 (\"Instant-on works with no battery or deeply discharged battery\") and p.19 (\"the system is regulated above the minimum system voltage (default 3.5 V)\"). With the module and the 3.3 V stage running the load is not zero, so the accepted range is 3.50 V up to 4.42 V (4.42 V is V(SYS(MAX)), BQ p.7); below 3.50 V is a fault. The simulation rows do not give a no-cell value for SYS.",
"   - REGN, pad 1 of C8 (net REGN): 4.7 V or more (BQ p.10, V(REGN) at V(VBUS) = 5 V and 20 mA: min 4.7 V, typ 4.8 V). The sheet gives no maximum at 5 V; it never exceeds 6.4 V (BQ p.10, maximum at 9 V). REGN reads only when the charger is awake on VBUS.",
"   - 3V3, pad 1 of C9 (net 3V3), the 3.3 V rail: 3.274 to 3.341 V (SN line 74, case5: \"3.274 V to 3.341 V with VFB plus or minus 1 percent\", nominal 3.3075 V, rows C5.1 and C5.2 in SL lines 126 and 127: 3.0 to 3.6 V is the module's own supply range, ES Table 6-2, VDD33 3.0 / 3.3 / 3.6 V). Outside 3.274 to 3.341 V but inside 3.0 to 3.6 V: the module is safe but report it; outside 3.0 to 3.6 V: switch off.",
"   - VRING, pad W1 (net VRING, the bottom-face pad marked \"W1 +\", no wire fitted): the ring supply is OFF in this test, because VRING_EN has R25 100 kohm to GND and nothing drives it (CS line 11, \"IO15 = VRING_EN (R25 100 kohm to GND)\") and the switch U5 is enabled by a high on EN (CN rows VRING_EN, U5 pin 3). Expected: about 0 V; accepted: under 0.1 V (0.1 V is the limit SL row R2.5 uses for a switched-off rail of the ring; no file gives a limit for VRING; for the lead). The current limit of VRING (401 mA, CS section 1 and SN line 70, case1) and the boost to VRING need the charger's boost mode and VRING_EN high: both need firmware, so they are NOT tested here.",
"   - Status light, the three lines (nets LED_R, LED_G, LED_B at pad 1 of R8, R9, R10; the module pins IO21, IO47, IO48 are not driven, CS line 103 onward): with no firmware the three module pins are not driven, and no file gives a voltage for the floating pin (CS section 9, \"Off states\": it only bounds a floating pin near 3.7 V at nanoampere level). So: the light is expected OFF, and the three pad voltages have no expected value in the files (for the lead). Hand test, one line at a time, meter in volts across the resistor: join the pad 1 of R8 to TP16 with a probe lead; the red LED lights, and the voltage across R8 (its two pads) is 1.53 V at 3.0 mA, the figure for SYS 3.5 V (CS lines 117 and 114: 3.0 mA at SYS 3.5 V, 4.6 mA at 4.35 V; 3.0 mA x 510 ohm = 1.53 V, 4.6 mA x 510 ohm = 2.35 V); accepted 1.53 to 2.35 V. Same for R9 (green) and R10 (blue): 2.2 mA x 270 ohm = 0.59 V at SYS 3.5 V up to 4.7 mA x 270 ohm = 1.27 V at 4.35 V (CS lines 118 and 119); accepted 0.59 to 1.27 V. Remove the lead after each. The simulation rows C4.1 to C4.4 in SL lines 122 to 125 name the older 470 and 150 ohm values and a 3V3 supply: they are before sim finding 3, so the CS section 9 table is used.",
"   - CHG_STAT, pad 1 of R24 (net CHG_STAT, open drain with R24 10 kohm to 3V3): high, about the 3V3 reading, because with CE held high \"HIGH indicates charge complete or charge disabled\" (BQ p.4, pin STAT; CS line 13, R24 10 kohm to 3V3). If STAT blinks at 1 Hz it reports a fault (same BQ p.4 entry): note it; with TH1 not yet fitted a fault is possible and no file says what the charger shows there (for the lead).",
"   - CHG_CE_N, pad 1 of R21 (net CHG_CE_N): high, about the 3V3 reading (R21 10 kohm to 3V3, CS line 103; the module pin is not driven).",
"   - TP1, net BOOT (module IO0): high, the 3V3 reading, through R22 10 kohm to 3V3 (CS line 11); the module's GPIO0 has a weak pull-up, bit value 1 (ES p.13, Table 4-1, \"GPIO0 Weak pull-up 1\"). Never join TP1 to ground while the module resets: that selects download mode (ES p.14, Table 4-3; not tested here).",
"   - TP2, net EN (module enable): high, the 3V3 reading: R23 10 kohm to 3V3 and C30 1 uF to GND (CS line 11), so it rises with a time constant of 10 ms (10 kohm x 1 uF, [CALC]); EN high means \"enables the chip\" (ES p.12, Table 3-1, pin EN: \"High: on, enables the chip\"). The module needs 50 us of stable supply before EN is high (ES p.16, Table 4-6, tSTBL). The module's high input level is 0.75 x VDD (ES Table 6-3): about 2.5 V at 3.3 V; accepted: at least 2.5 V.",
"   - TP3, net TXD0 (module pin 39, UART0 transmit), scope or a USB to UART adapter, after the supply is raised: ROM code messages are printed at boot. Quote: ES p.15, section 4.3: \"During boot process the messages by the ROM code can be printed to: (Default) UART0 and USB Serial/JTAG controller\". No saved Espressif document in design/datasheets gives the text of the message, the baud rate or the delay of the first message (search of case_esp32s3_mini1.pdf and case_esp32s3_ds.pdf for \"115200\", \"baud\", \"rst:0x\", \"invalid header\": no hit, W/txt/ and W/search_boot.log), so those are left out. Expected and accepted: some activity (pulses) on TP3 within the first seconds after the supply is raised, and that is all the files support; a TP3 that stays silent is a fault or an eFuse set to silence the ROM (ES p.15: the printing \"can be ... disabled by configuring registers and eFuse\").",
"   - TP4, net RXD0 (module pin 40, UART0 receive): no expected value in the files (no external part on the net; the pin's idle state is not stated in CS or ES). For the lead.",
"   - TP5, net QON (charger pin 12): the charger's internal pull-up: 4.3 V typical at V(VBUS) = 5 V (BQ p.10, V(QON) \"V(VBUS) = 5 V ... 4.3 V\"; internal resistance 200 kohm, so use a meter of 10 Mohm or more). The sheet gives no minimum or maximum: expected about 4.3 V, no accepted range in the files (for the lead).",
"   - TP6 to TP15, nets SPARE_IO33 to SPARE_IO42 (module IO33 to IO42, CS line 11): no part on these nets, the module pins are not driven: no expected value in the files (for the lead). Unpowered only, meter in resistance against TP16: a steady reading below about 50 ohm is a short (the limit of step 9, lead).",
"   - TP16, net GND: the reference of every reading above (0 V by definition). Unpowered, resistance from TP16 to a GND pad of J1 (A1): must beep (CS line 15); the files give no value in ohm.",
"   - Hidden joint group H1, the module U1 supply and ground pads (pin 3 = 3V3, pins 1, 2, 42, 43 and 46 to 65 = GND, CS line 11; the nine centre pads are tied by the copper pattern of Round 2, \"Planes\"): shown by TP3 only, with the 3V3 reading in range. A missing pin 3 joint, or no ground joint at all, leaves the module unpowered or unreferenced: TP3 stays silent while the 3V3 pad of C9 still reads in range (the rail is made by U3, not by the module). One missing ground joint among the many does not change any reading: NOT proven.",
"   - Hidden joint group H2, the module's signal pads: only pin 39 (TXD0) is shown, by TP3 activity. EN (pin 45) is not proven: the TP2 reading is taken on the R23 side of the net and would read high with the module pin joint open. The test pads TP1, TP4 to TP15 reach the module pins by track, so their readings do not prove the module-side joints either. The module pins IO0 to IO48 other than TXD0: NOT proven (no firmware to drive and read them).",
"   - Hidden joint group H3, the charger U2's edge pads: SYS at 3.50 to 3.65 V proves that the VBUS (pin 1), SW (19, 20), SYS (15, 16) and PGND (17, 18) paths conduct (at least one joint of each pair: the reading does not change when only one of two pins is open); REGN at 4.7 V or more proves the REGN pin 22 joint and that the charger runs on 5 V. BAT (13, 14) is NOT proven: no cell is fitted, and no file gives the BAT pad voltage with no battery (pad W5; no expected value in the files, for the lead). STAT, CE: the readings above are taken on the R24 and R21 side; they prove those parts, not the charger pad joints. A missing VBUS or SYS joint shows as SYS far below 3.50 V (near 0 V) with the supply showing normal VBUS_IN.",
"   - Hidden joint group H4, the charger's centre pad (pad 25, ground and thermal; four paste windows, PM P2): this test does NOT prove it. The charger's ground is also carried by the PGND pins 17 and 18, so SYS and REGN read correctly with the centre pad not soldered at all. The test does not measure how much solder covers the pad, voids, or the heat path. The files give no test for it (for the lead; an X-ray or a section would be needed, PM P3 table, \"unverified\").",
"   - What this test does NOT prove, in one place: solder coverage under the charger's centre pad; every module signal joint except TXD0; one missing ground joint among the module's many; the charger's BAT pins; the charging path and the charge current; the VRING switch U5 and its 401 mA limit and the boost; the status light's drive from the module (only the LED, resistor and LED supply path is shown by the hand test); the battery thermistor path (TH1 is fitted at step 10); the SD NAND U4 (no firmware reads it); the USB data lines. All of these need the cell or firmware and belong to a later test.",
]
L[i1 + 1:i1 + 1] = B
open(F, "w", encoding="utf-8").write("\n".join(L))
print("ok", len(B))
